							</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<td height="50" colspan="3" valign="top" class="windowbg2" align="left">Okay, fill me in on another thing I&#039;m out of the loop on.&nbsp; I noticed today there&#039;s a new option on the character select screen called &quot;Return Home&quot;.&nbsp; If I select it, my character can be sent to his home city.&nbsp; So umm.... Origin AA that I paid 7 AA for?&nbsp; That got the most use for me at the end of the night or beginning of the next play session.&nbsp; So now all I&#039;d have to do is wait til the next time I played and just hit &quot;return home&quot;.&nbsp; <br /><br />In other news, the veteran abilities look kind of neat.&nbsp; I think I have 5 of those coming.&nbsp; Haven&#039;t bothered to check yet... <br />But to give it a pessimistic slant, it&#039;s more proof EQ finally gave up on getting new subscribers.&nbsp; The game already has a big learning curve because of the size of the world, the complication level of the game (gazillion or so different windows now), and the heavily ingrained EQ culture that a new player would know nothing about.&nbsp; Then there&#039;s the sheer time necessary to even begin to measure up to L70s with what many consider essential AAs now.&nbsp; So longtime players already have a very heavy advantage, which is fine.&nbsp; But if you want new subscribers, you don&#039;t want to add even more inherent advantages for long time players.&nbsp; The nature of the game provides so many already.&nbsp; But hey, that&#039;s not unexpected.&nbsp; EQ is hardly going to get any new subscribers at this point.<br /><br />/ramble on</td>
